SUMMARY:Seeing the invisible: Using light as a tool of discovery (Dr George
  Smetana)
LOCATION:Poynting Large Lecture Theatre\, Physics Building (R13)
DESCRIPTION:Dr George Smetana\nInstitute of Physics Public Lecture Series\n
 \nThe human body is a surprisingly good instrument for scientific discover
 y\, with our senses able to act as probes of the physics going on all arou
 nd us. However\, over time we've reached the limit of our experience livin
 g at an 'average' scale - too big to easily experience quantum phenomena a
 nd too small to comprehend the workings of the universe on cosmic scales. 
 We've developed tools to help us\, and the detection and manipulation of l
 ight has become one of the most important ones. I will share with you some
  of the surprising ways that we can use light in modern physics\, even to 
 detect the invisible.\n\nDetails: https://www.birmingham.ac.uk/about/colle
 ge-of-engineering-and-physical-sciences/physics-and-astronomy/outreach-and
 -schools-liaison/outreach-for-secondary-schools/iop-public-lecture-series
CATEGORIES:Institute of Physics Public Lecture Series
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://www.birmingham.ac.uk/about/college-of-engineering-and-physical-
 sciences/physics-and-astronomy/outreach-and-schools-liaison/outreach-for-s
 econdary-schools/iop-public-lecture-series
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iop-20270413T1930@talks.birmingham.prototype
DTSTAMP:20261128T102803Z
DTSTART:20270413T183000Z
DTEND:20270413T193000Z
SUMMARY:How to Film the Life of an Electron (Dr Tom Siday)
LOCATION:Poynting Large Lecture Theatre\, Physics Building (R13)
DESCRIPTION:Dr Tom Siday\nInstitute of Physics Public Lecture Series\n\nEv
 erything around you — your phone\, solar panels\, computers\, the screen
  you're reading this on — works because of electrons zipping around insi
 de materials. But catching them in the act is absurdly hard: they move in 
 millionths of a billionth of a second\, across distances smaller than a si
 ngle atom. No ordinary camera comes close.In this talk\, I'll show how we 
 are building what amounts to the world's fastest and best-resolved camera\
 , using some of the shortest possible flashes of light to photograph matte
 r atom by atom at their native timescales. We're getting close to a long-s
 tanding dream: recording real-time movies of the fastest processes in natu
 re.\n\nDetails: https://www.birmingham.ac.uk/about/college-of-engineering-
 and-physical-sciences/physics-and-astronomy/outreach-and-schools-liaison/o
 utreach-for-secondary-schools/iop-public-lecture-series
CATEGORIES:Institute of Physics Public Lecture Series
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://www.birmingham.ac.uk/about/college-of-engineering-and-physical-
 sciences/physics-and-astronomy/outreach-and-schools-liaison/outreach-for-s
 econdary-schools/iop-public-lecture-series
